-- title:
--   Generalized dominated convergence with a moving dominant
-- statement:
--   Let $(X, \mu)$ be an arbitrary measure space and let
--
--   $$g_n,\,G_n : X \to [0,\infty], \qquad g_\infty,\,G_\infty : X \to [0,\infty] \qquad (n \in \mathbb{N})$$
--
--   be measurable functions with values in the extended non-negative reals. Assume:
--
--   * $g_n \le G_n$ pointwise, for every $n$;
--   * $g_n \to g_\infty$ pointwise $\mu$-almost everywhere;
--   * $G_n \to G_\infty$ pointwise $\mu$-almost everywhere;
--   * $\displaystyle\int_X G_n \,d\mu = \int_X G_\infty \,d\mu$ for every $n$;
--   * $\displaystyle\int_X G_\infty\,d\mu < \infty$.
--
--   Then
--
--   $$\limsup_{n \to \infty} \int_X g_n \,d\mu \;\le\; \int_X g_\infty \,d\mu .$$
--
--   This is the Vitali, or "moving dominant", form of the dominated convergence theorem: the dominating functions $G_n$ are allowed to depend on $n$, and the only thing asked of them is that their integrals be constant (equivalently, that they converge in the sense of integrals to their almost-everywhere limit) and finite. It is the tool that makes an approximation argument work when no single integrable majorant is available but a family of majorants with an exactly preserved integral is — for instance when the dominants are conditional expectations of a fixed integrable function on a shrinking family of partitions.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** All functions take values in `ℝ≥0∞` and all integrals are lower Lebesgue integrals, so no integrability side conditions beyond the stated finiteness are needed and no subtraction of infinities can occur. The measure $\mu$ is completely arbitrary — in particular it need not be $\sigma$-finite — and the conclusion is a bound on the $\limsup$ only, not a convergence statement.
